tools/pipeline/ledger.json:21027:   "state_note": "*** DO NOT CLOSE THIS ROW ON THE EXISTENCE OF `state_semantics`.  I ALMOST DID, 2026-08-29. ***\n\nThe block is written, all five observed states are documented (`closed`/`held`/`open`/\n`in work`/`dropped` -- census run 2026-08-29, 270 rows, none undocumented), and `_ADOPTED`\nsays the owner ratified it on 2026-08-25 with both amendments.  **That is the SEMANTICS half\nand it IS discharged.**  It is also the visible half, which is why it reads like the whole row.\n\n**The row's own `hold` names the other half: ~50 mis-stated instances across five classes,\nand it is ITS OWN RECORDS-CORRECTION ROUND** -- which additionally owes the standing second\nreview pass, because corrections repeat the errors they correct.  None of that is done.\n\nSo the state is right and the row is easy to misread as finished.  Recorded here because the\ncheapest defence against a wrong closure is a sentence at the place the closure gets made.\n\nPASS-1 PANEL: `_scratchpad/panel_20260825_030653_matrix`. SIX SEATS FIRED, FIVE ANSWERED, ONE DOWN. grok, codex, minimax-m3, kimi-k3:cloud and glm-5.2 answered; **agy FAILED on a SIXTH seat-failure class -- its own streaming transport dropped mid-answer** (`connection to the agent was interrupted ... subscriber fell behind updates, stalled for 6s`) -- and was re-fired, since a dropped connection is worth retrying where a wall is not. *** grok's verdict is the headline: DO NOT TRUST STATES. *** THE FINDINGS, by class. (1) STATE CONTRADICTS SUBSTANCE: `pitlatch` and `pitclobber` sit `in work` while every closure note says CLOSE and the residual their hold names is already discharged by a CLOSED carrier; `hpcshcpu` is `open` while its own note says DROP FROM THE ACTIONABLE QUEUE; `m8invpred` is `held` behind a codex wall discharged 2026-08-20; `reprowitness` is `in work` although the witness ladder it produced has been in the carrier since 2026-08-19; `gdromdmawidth` is `open` while admitting shipped code carries a comment under its name. (2) *** THE BURIAL SHAPE, TEXTBOOK FORM: `b115` and `b126` EACH CARRY TWO INDEPENDENT DEFECTS UNDER ONE ID. *** Closing either on one defect buries the other -- the exact hazard the carrier records. This is the highest-severity finding here. (3) STALE BLOCKERS: `gdromdmawidth` waits on `sh4gdromexit`, a carrier that will never do the work attributed to it. (4) UNEVIDENCED CLOSES: `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault`, `witnessinvisible` close asserting shipped substance with no commits; `b75` is a contested close with no recorded adjudication; `fbwitness` closes with an absent witness while its title claims a probe. (5) A CORRECTION THAT DID NOT PROPAGATE: a measured 4x inflation was corrected in `arcbiosubsan` and never in `sgiarcbiosoob`, which still asserts it -- mechanical rule 2 (grep for a claim's SIBLINGS before calling it fixed) failing in the ledger itself. *** AND A WARNING FOR WHOEVER TAKES THIS ROUND, because the round that filed it got this wrong THREE TIMES: THE LEDGER HAS MULTIPLE SCHEMA ERAS AND A CHECKER THAT KNOWS ONE ERA INVENTS ANOMALIES. *** (a) Narrative lives in `entries` on 198 older rows and `state_note` on 19 newer ones; counting only the latter reported 191 rows with no narrative when the true number is ZERO. (b) `witness` is FORWARD-ONLY from 2026-08-19 (`CLAUDE.md:1191`), so of 16 closed-without-witness rows FOURTEEN are compliant and only `rtcdet` and `fbwitness` are owed. (c) `closure.by` postdates part of the ledger. (d) 'open/held WITH commits' looked like 16 anomalies and is ZERO -- all sixteen carry a HOLD marker, which is the normal batching state by design. **Establish the validity rule for each class BEFORE touching a cell, and print a denominator beside every count.**  *** THE SCOPE WAS MEASURED AND IT IS 12 ROWS, NOT ~50. The ~50 figure this row was FILED with was my own, and it was wrong for the FIFTH time in one session, by the same mechanism every previous time. *** Establishing each class's validity rule BEFORE counting -- which is what the adjudication demanded -- collapses the raw counts hard: A closed-without-witness 16 raw -> **2** (14 predate the 2026-08-19 forward-only cutoff and are COMPLIANT); B closed-without-commits 6 raw -> **3** (three are withdrawn / not-a-bug / documented-only); C open-or-held-with-commits 17 raw -> **1** (fifteen carry a HOLD, the normal batching state, and one was `asicbadaddr` itself, closed when #463 committed); D closed-without-closure.by 2 raw -> **2**; E state-contradicts-closure 30 raw -> **5**. *** RULE 5, AND IT IS THE ONE THAT COST THE MOST: `closure.note` MAY DISPOSE OF A STAGE, NOT THE ROW. *** The ledger defines four `phase_labels` -- assess, research, review, regress -- so `CLOSE (assess)` closes the ASSESS STAGE and the row legitimately stays `held`. TWENTY-FIVE of class E's thirty are that shape; `exitsweep`'s own hold says so out loud (*\"CLOSURE REVERTED ... the fable5 batch answered this row's held stage\"*). Reading a stage-scoped disposition as a row-scoped one manufactured 25 false defects. THE TWELVE THAT SURVIVE: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmadefault, witnessinvisible, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m437ord, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, cmtattr, hpcshcpu -- plus this row. Note the convergence worth trusting: `fbwitness`, `rtcdet`, `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` were named INDEPENDENTLY by the reading seats AND fall out of the mechanical census, which is the only kind of agreement that counts here.  RECONCILIATION, demanded by the adjudicator before either number may be quoted: the class column sums to **13 instances** and the repair set is **11 rows**. 13 - 1 dedup (`fbwitness` is in BOTH closed-no-witness and closed-no-commits) - 1 self (`ledgerstatelies` is this tracking row) = 11. Both numbers are correct; the arithmetic was simply never shown, which made the correction READ as contradicting its own column -- **a sixth counting defect sitting inside the correction that reports the fifth.**  *** THE ROUND'S CENTRAL FINDING, MEASURED, AND IT IS NOT WHAT EITHER SEAT CLAIMED: `state` IS NOT DERIVABLE FROM THE RECORD. *** Two file-reading seats disagreed on `fbwitness` -- one said `CLOSE (assess)` disposes of a STAGE so the row must be `held`, the other said it retires the assessment so `closed` is fine. Settled empirically rather than by vote, and BOTH inferences are unsupported: **23 rows carry the IDENTICAL `closure.note` of `CLOSE (assess)` and they are spread across THREE states -- 12 `held`, 7 `in work`, 4 `closed`** (the closed four being ns16550, fablequeue, carrier, fbwitness). The obvious second hypothesis was tested and also fails: the number of STAGES with recorded entries is statistically identical across the three groups (mean 1.8 / 1.8 / 1.9, same min and max), so stage coverage does not explain the split either. **`state` and `closure.note` are independent fields with no enforced relationship**, so a reader cannot derive one from the other and neither can a checker. That is why 'DO NOT TRUST STATES' is the correct verdict, and it reframes this round: **the repair cannot be a mechanical rewrite of 11 cells, because there is no rule to rewrite them TO.** The round must first establish what `state` MEANS -- most plausibly 'is there work outstanding on this row', which is a different question from 'has any stage been adjudicated' -- and only then repair. ALSO REFUTED HERE, and worth keeping because it was checkable in two minutes: the claim that `fbwitness` is ALONE among its 2026-08-20 cohort in being closed. That cohort is 84 rows -- 17 closed, 43 held, 11 dropped, 13 in work -- and four of the closed share `fbwitness`'s exact closure string. A seat's argument from 'this row is the only one' is worth checking every time; this one was wrong.  *** FIVE SEATS, ELEVEN ROWS, AND THE DISAGREEMENT IS THE EVIDENCE RATHER THAN THE NOISE. *** agy, kimi-k3, glm-5.2, minimax-m3 and grok each assigned a correct state to all eleven rows, reading the same ledger under the same four validity rules stated in the brief. **They agree on only 4 of 11.** `hpcshcpu` drew THREE different answers (closed / dropped / held); six more drew two. This is what a field with no derivable meaning looks like from the outside: competent independent readers do not converge, because there is nothing to converge ON. *** IT ALSO PARTITIONS THE ROUND CLEANLY, WHICH IS THE USEFUL PART. *** **UNANIMOUS (4) -- and all four are ALREADY IN THE RIGHT STATE**: `gdromdmadefault` and `witnessinvisible` (closed, missing `commits`), `m437ord` (closed, missing `closure.by`), `cmtattr` (held, correct as-is). What these need is FIELDS, not state changes, and a field that is absent is absent under any definition of `state` -- so they are repairable NOW, independently of the rule question. **SPLIT (7)**: rtcdet, fbwitness, gdromdmawidth, gate3scope, m8invpred, ledgerwitness, hpcshcpu. Every one of these turns on what `state` MEANS, and none can be repaired until that is settled -- editing them now would be manufacturing the appearance of correctness by picking whichever seat I found most persuasive. **THE SPLIT IS ALSO A CHECK ON THE 23-ROW FINDING**, arrived at from the opposite direction: that one measured one closure string against three states; this one measures five readers against one row set. Both say the same thing and neither depends on the other's method.  *** ADJUDICATED 2026-08-25, AND IT REFRAMED THE ROUND WHILE CONVICTING ME OF ONE OVERREACH. *** The ruling: `state` and `closure.note` are different TENSES. The note is a PAST-TENSE event log of what happened to a STAGE; `state` is a PRESENT-TENSE assertion of what the ROW still OWES. **Under that reading the multi-state spread of one closure string is the ledger WORKING AS DESIGNED, not the defect** -- rows that experienced the same stage event legitimately differ in what else they owe -- and the stage-coverage test failing is CORROBORATION rather than a second mystery, because state depends on what REMAINS, not on how much happened. **SO THE THING TO FILE IS THE MISSING SCHEMA SEMANTICS, NOT THE SPREAD.** That single absence is the cause behind six mis-counts in one session, two seats inferring opposite conventions from one string, and five seats agreeing on only 4 of 11 rows. The fix is written: a `state_semantics` block now sits in the ledger's own schema, PENDING OWNER RATIFICATION, forward-only in the same way `witness` was. *** MY OVERREACH, RETRACTED IN PLACE: I wrote that 'for most of the 11 there is no fact of the matter to restore -- only a decision'. THAT IS FALSE AND IT WAS CONVENIENT FOR ME*** -- it converts eleven tedious substance reads into one interesting rule question. What died was a repair METHOD (derive state from the note), not the fact of the matter, which is read from commits, gates, holds and filed questions -- exactly how `asicbadaddr` was closed, with no note convention needed. **The seminar is over in one paragraph; the eleven reads remain owed.** *** AND NO NUMBER IN THIS ROW MAY BE QUOTED AS A PREMISE UNTIL A SECOND PARTY CHECKS IT. *** I have mis-counted this ledger six times in one session. Every ruling above depends on the EXISTENCE of the spread, not its size -- ONE held row and ONE closed row sharing a closure string already prove non-derivability -- so the whole second-party check is two greps. The figures 23, 84 and the 1.8/1.8/1.9 stage means are UNVERIFIED SUPPORTING COLOUR, labelled as such, and are leads rather than premises.  *** A SEVENTH COUNTING HAZARD, FOUND BY MAKING IT: SEAT NAMES ARE NOT SEAT ANSWERS. *** While closing #463's stages I reported research at 9/9; it was 8/9. The `codex` entry on that stage is a **`[HELD AWAITING SEAT:` MARKER** -- a record that the seat is OUTSTANDING -- and I counted it because I counted seat NAMES. `check_stage_panels.py` excludes marker entries deliberately, and its own comment says exactly why: *'otherwise marking a gap would close it.'* **Writing down that a seat is missing would, under a name-count, make the stage look complete** -- the seat-failure-as-agreement shape in its purest mechanical form. **THE LEDGER CARRIES 44 SUCH MARKER ENTRIES**, each one a seat that has NOT answered. Any future census of panel coverage that counts names rather than answers over-reports by up to that much. Three marker tokens exist and all three must be excluded: `[HELD AWAITING SEAT:`, `[NOT A PANEL STAGE: SINGLE-SEAT RE-READ]`, and `[RUN UNDER THE PRE-2026-08-16 POLICY]`. *This belongs with the schema-semantics finding rather than beside it: `entries` is not a list of answers, it is a list of EVENTS, and some events record an absence. That is the same present-tense/past-tense confusion that `state` and `closure.note` embody, one level down.*  *** A NEW INSTANCE OF THIS ROW'S OWN DEFECT, FOUND 2026-08-25 BY THE ADJUDICATOR TRIPPING OVER IT WHILE ADJUDICATING THIS VERY BLOCK. *** Asked to rule on the queue, the fable5 seat reported that `idesync` \"is already closed as document-do-not-fix\". The ledger says `state = \"in work\"` with `commits` EMPTY. Both readings are half right, and the shape is new: *** THE DISPOSITION IS SETTLED BUT THE DOCUMENT HAS NOT SHIPPED. *** The row carries an opus5 REVIEW verdict (2026-08-20) of DOCUMENT-DO-NOT-FIX on a deciding fact nobody else raised -- the drive never advertises FLUSH CACHE, so the highest IDENTIFY word written never sets it -- plus a closed assess stage (`closure.by = fable5`, `CLOSE (assess)`). A SUBSTANCE reader therefore sees a finished decision and says \"closed\"; a STATE reader sees no commits and says \"unfinished\". Under the semantics now written, `closed` means discharged BY WORK, and a documenting commit IS work that has not happened -- so the state field is RIGHT and the adjudicator was WRONG, while the adjudicator was right about the only thing anyone would act on. *** THE LESSON IS THAT `document-do-not-fix` NEEDS ITS OWN DISCHARGE, AND NOTHING TRACKS IT. *** A row in this state is invisible to both readings: it looks decided to anyone reading the verdict and claimed to anyone reading the state, so nobody ships the document. That is an UNDISCHARGED OBLIGATION with no carrier -- the class section I of precommit exists for, one field over. Candidate fix, NOT applied pending ratification: `closed` requires either a commit or an explicit `documented-only` closure badge, so an assessed-not-changed record cannot close without the record existing. The project's ethos already demands honest 'assessed, not changed' records; what is missing is anything that NOTICES when one was promised and never written.\n\n*** THE HUNT FOR A CONSUMED DECISION IS NO LONGER EMPTY.  2026-09-08, THREE FRESH INSTANCES, ONE OF\nWHICH COST REAL WORK. ***\n\nThis row was filed noting that mis-stated states had cost audit expense and reader distrust but\n**zero decisions**.  That is no longer true, and the instances are recorded here rather than in the\nrounds that found them, so the census stays in one place.\n\n**CLASS 1 (STATE CONTRADICTS SUBSTANCE) -- and this one CONSUMED A DECISION.**\n`sgirergbroundtrip` sat `open`, `commits: None`, `witness: None` while **`#505` had already fixed\nit** -- the tree carries the corrected GET arm AND a `#505:` provenance comment above it, and\n`CHANGELOG.md` carries a full round block.  I read the row's title at face value while scoping the\n`sgirandomcolour` round in the same file and **began designing around a defect that was already\ngone.**  Caught only by opening the source for an unrelated reason.  *Closed 2026-09-08 with its\ncommit and witness.*\n\n**A NEIGHBOURING CLASS THIS ROW DOES NOT YET NAME: MISSING COMMIT ATTRIBUTION ON A MULTI-ROUND\nCOMMIT.**  `7a41b3f` is titled *\"#505 #506 #507 #508: four test-first rounds\"* and **not one of the\nfour rows carried its commit**:\n\n    sgirergbroundtrip  #505   state stale AND commits None AND witness None   <- worst\n    lcaconfwidth       #506   commits None\n    alphaswpctxptbr    #507   commits None\n    alphapfnoffset     #508   commits None\n\nEach verified before correcting: the commit's diff touches the right source file and carries the\nmatching round tag (`#506` x6 in `dev_lca.c`, `#507` x6 in `cpu_alpha_palcode.c`, `#508` x4 in\n`memory_alpha.c`).  All three attributions restored; **states deliberately left alone**, since\n`in work` may be correct for a row with residual obligations and changing it would be a claim I had\nnot checked.\n\n> **THE MECHANISM, and it is why this class had not appeared before: a commit shipping SEVERAL\n> rounds is ONE act to the committer and N rows to the ledger, so the per-row field is what falls\n> out.  A single-round commit cannot fail this way.**  That is a different generator from the five\n> classes already enumerated here, and it predicts the failure recurs at exactly the next\n> multi-round commit.\n\n**HOW THEY WERE FOUND, in order, because the order is the lesson:** the state gap BY ACCIDENT\n(opening source while scoping something else); then the three attribution gaps by asking the general\nquestion -- *\"which non-closed rows are named by a CHANGELOG round-block heading?\"* -- which is\nprecise because the heading format is fixed (`# \\`#NNN\\` <rowid>: <title>`).  **That query returned\n7 candidates of which 4 were legitimate `in work` rows**, so it is a candidate list needing\nrow-by-row verification and NOT a population claim.  *This row's own history is a warning against\nquoting a count here, so none is quoted.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31227:   "state_note": "*** MY OWN DIAGNOSIS IN THIS ROW WAS WRONG WITHIN THE HOUR, AND THE CORRECTION MATTERS BECAUSE IT CHANGES\nTHE REMEDY. ***  I wrote that the trigger is 'the SUBJECT SHAPE, not a phrase', on the evidence of two\nrefusals of one brief.  **codex then answered a brief on THE SAME DEFECT, in the same file, at 167 KB.**\n\nThe difference is the FRAMING, and it is sharper than either earlier guess:\n  REFUSED  the WITNESS brief -- 'can you build a probe that REACHES this and shows the bad value?'\n  ANSWERED the DESIGN brief -- 'what should this code do instead, and what should the rows assert?'\nSame defect, same file, same session.  A request to CONSTRUCT A PATH TO A DEFECT reads as an attack recipe;\na request to DECIDE WHAT THE CODE SHOULD DO reads as ordinary review.  Neutralising the vocabulary did not\nhelp because the vocabulary was never the trigger.\n\n**SO THE REMEDY IS NOT ONLY RE-ROUTING: it is to ask codex the DESIGN question and let another seat answer\nthe REACHABILITY one.**  That is cheaper than it sounds -- grok answered the witness brief with a complete\nbuildable sketch, and codex then produced the best design answer of the panel.  Each seat was asked the\nquestion it would answer.\n\n*** THE SEAT-CHECK HAZARD IS UNCHANGED AND IS THE PART THAT WAS ACTUALLY FIXED. ***  The refusal writes a\nzero-byte stdout with the reason on stderr on a direct call -- indistinguishable from an agy quota wall --\nand through panel.sh it produced 150,372 bytes at 97% novel, which `seat_verdicts.py` scored as **answered,\nNO VERDICT LINE**.  A seat failure counted as an answer, in the tool built to refuse exactly that.  Every\nexisting test passed it: no quota keyword (not a wall), high novelty because the seat streams reasoning\nBEFORE the filter fires (not an echo), CLI seat (not a burn).  Now reported as **SEAT REFUSED**, counted with\nthe DOWN seats, keyed on the vendor's own sentence, and controlled in four directions: it fires on the\nrefusal, leaves a genuine quota wall as WALLED, leaves the mid-answer wall's READ-THE-BODY label intact, and\ndoes not touch answering seats.\n\nBoth refusal transcripts and the answered design brief are preserved beside the round's artifacts, so the\nbefore/after comparison is checkable rather than remembered.\n\n*** THIRD INSTANCE, 2026-09-07 -- AND IT REFUTES THE DIAGNOSIS THIS ROW CURRENTLY CARRIES. ***\n\nPreserved: `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/refusals/attempt3_scoping.err'.  Exact text, twice:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: https://chatgpt.com/cyber\n\n**stdout was 0 bytes; the banner, the echoed prompt and both ERROR lines went to stderr** -- so a\ncheck that measures the `.txt' alone sees an empty file and a check that measures SIZE sees 6829\nbytes of healthy-looking stderr.  `seat_verdicts.py' already reports this as SEAT REFUSED / DOWN\n(fixed earlier in this session); this instance confirms the detector on a fresh case.\n\n*** WHAT IS REFUTED: this row concluded the trigger is the reach-the-defect FRAMING -- *design\nbriefs are answered, witness briefs are refused*.  THE REFUSED BRIEF HERE IS NEITHER. ***  It is a\nread-only SCOPING task that explicitly says *\"Do not design the fix diff.  Do not write rows\"*.\n\n**AND THE CONTROL IS STRONG, because it ran the same evening on the same model at the same effort:**\nthe `sgirebufuninit' pass-1 brief -- which discusses uninitialised host stack reaching guest-visible\nmemory, i.e. a MEMORY-DISCLOSURE defect -- was ANSWERED at 317 KB with a GO-WITH-CHANGES verdict and\ntwo new findings.  So the trigger is not the subject matter, not the security-adjacent content, and\nnot the brief's purpose.\n\n**THE REMAINING HYPOTHESIS, stated as a hypothesis because it is not yet tested: VOCABULARY DENSITY.**\nThe refused brief is saturated with this fork's historical term for the defect -- *kills*, *THE HOST\nSTILL DIES*, *terminating calls*, *host kill* -- while the answered brief describes an equivalent\nseverity in ordinary engineering words.  A re-fire is in flight with the identical technical content\nrewritten in bounds-and-error-handling language (`RESEARCH_scoping_neutral_20260907.md'): same\nquestions, same code, same seven dispositions, no `kill'/`dies'/`host' vocabulary.\n\n**That is a real experiment with a clean control and it settles the hypothesis either way** -- if the\nneutral form answers, the trigger is vocabulary and the remedy is the benign-framing rule the carrier\nalready mandates for the Anthropic seats, applied to codex too; if it also refuses, vocabulary is out\nand the next candidate is brief LENGTH or the enumerate-the-sites structure.\n\n*Note the cost is real and recurring: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, and a refusal is\ninvisible to any size-based check.*\n\n*** SOLVED, WITH A CONTROLLED EXPERIMENT.  THE TRIGGER IS THE REPOSITORY'S OWN ARCHIVES, NOT THE BRIEF'S\nSUBJECT AND NOT ITS FRAMING.  Measured 2026-09-07 across four runs. ***\n\n    attempt  brief vocabulary   reading scope        stderr     flag\n    1        kills / HOST DIES  unrestricted           6,829 b  IMMEDIATE, before any work\n    3        neutral            unrestricted         374,583 b  at byte 279,268 (~75% in)\n    4        neutral            src/ + regress/*.py  279,132 b  *** NONE -- full verdict delivered ***\n    (control) pass-2 panel      unrestricted         395,340 b  at the END of the answer\n\n**TWO SEPARATE FILTERS, and each attempt isolates one:**\n* **Attempt 1 -> 3 shows a PROMPT-level filter keyed on vocabulary.**  Rewriting *kills / the host dies /\n  terminating calls* into ordinary bounds-and-error-handling language converted an instant refusal into a run\n  that worked for minutes.  The technical content was IDENTICAL -- same twelve sites, same expectation design,\n  same seven dispositions.\n* **Attempt 3 -> 4 shows a CONTENT-level filter keyed on what the seat READS.**  Adding one paragraph -- *do\n  not read `regress/artifacts/' or `tools/pipeline/ledger.json'* -- carried the run past the exact point where\n  the unrestricted one died, to a clean finish.  **279,132 vs 279,268 bytes: the same distance, opposite\n  outcome.**\n\n**WHY THE ARCHIVES: they are this project's own record of a hardening campaign** -- kill tables, `host kill'\ncensuses, `exit(1)' enumerations, mutant names like `widthkillclass' -- accumulated over months.  Read in bulk\nthey look like exactly what the filter exists to catch.  *The irony is that the archive is the honest record\nof DEFENSIVE work, and its own vocabulary is what makes the seat refuse to read it.*\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE, and it costs two sentences in a brief: for any codex run, (a) write the brief in\n> ordinary engineering language, and (b) SCOPE ITS READING to `src/` and `regress/*.py`.**  The pass-2 control\n> shows why (b) matters even when (a) is satisfied: that brief was already neutral and was still flagged, at\n> the end, after reading the ledger and CHANGELOG.\n\nThis matters more than one lost answer: codex is the seat this queue leans on hardest, a refusal writes 0\nbytes to stdout while leaving healthy-looking stderr, and it is invisible to every size-based check.\n\n*** SIXTH RUN, AND IT REFUTES THE REMEDY I SHIPPED TWO HOURS AGO -- SCOPING IS NECESSARY, NOT\nSUFFICIENT.  Worse: it REHABILITATES the diagnosis I recorded as REFUTED, and my refutation was a\nmisreading of my own brief. ***\n\nA `swpctx' design brief scoped ENTIRELY to `src/' was flagged at 145 KB -- the flag fired while it was\ngrepping `debugger_cmds.c', which is INSIDE the allowed scope.  All six runs, sorted by what they asked:\n\n    WHAT IS TRUE                                    WHAT TO BUILD\n    analysis of a defect        317 KB clean        smallest EDIT that RE-OPENS     375 KB FLAGGED\n    count + classify            298 KB clean        smallest guest PROGRAM that\n    is this path reached?       294 KB clean          WITNESSES each defect         145 KB FLAGGED\n    CAN a guest run at all?     502 KB clean\n\n**The discriminator is whether the brief asks the seat to BUILD SOMETHING THAT TRIGGERS A DEFECT.**  The\n502 KB clean run asked at length about cold-debugger probes, breakpoints and planting instructions -- and\nwas fine, because it asked whether such a thing is POSSIBLE rather than for one aimed at a defect.\n\n*** AND I HAD THIS RIGHT THE FIRST TIME. ***  This row's original diagnosis was *the reach-the-defect\nFRAMING is the trigger*.  I recorded it REFUTED because a read-only SCOPING brief was also refused --\nbut that brief's Q3 asked *\"what is the smallest change to the model that would restore the old\nbehaviour ... while still satisfying a test built that way?\"*  **That is construction framing, and I did\nnot re-read my own brief before declaring the diagnosis dead.**  The premise-audit failure again, on my\nown record rather than on the code.\n\n**THE ARCHIVE FINDING IS NOT SUPERSEDED** -- it carried a run past the exact byte where an unscoped one\ndied, which is a measurement and stands.  There are TWO triggers; §C5 shipped implying one, and now says\nso.\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it costs something real:** codex cannot be asked for mutants or witness\nprograms.  It can CHECK a construction supplied to it, and it can say whether one is possible -- both\nmeasured clean.  **The construction itself goes to a measuring seat.**  That is a genuine capability\nloss, not a wording problem, and it should be weighed on the 9th alongside [[codexbriefscoping]].\n\n*** SEVENTH RUN, AND A THIRD REFINEMENT: A BRIEF THAT ASKS `IS THIS TRUE' CAN STILL BE REFUSED IF ITS\nCONTENT IS AN EXPLOITATION CHAIN.  Immediate rejection at 8 KB. ***\n\nThe `sgilinearnegidx' verification brief obeyed both existing rules: neutral engineering language, reading\nscoped to `src/' and `regress/*.py', and it asked only for each link of a chain to be CHECKED -- explicitly\n*\"I want it attacked rather than confirmed\"*, with no construction requested anywhere.  **Refused on the\nprompt**, before any work, exactly like the first `kills'-vocabulary attempt.\n\n**What it contained that the six clean runs did not: a numbered seven-step guest-controlled chain ending in\na PHYSICAL write, plus questions about the reachable address range and about how far the guest can CONTROL\nthe value.**  The clean runs analysed defects, classified sites, traced reachability of a code path, and\nasked whether a probe is possible.  None of them laid out *guest does A, then B, then C, and thereby writes\nwhere it chooses*.\n\n> **So the discriminator is not the QUESTION FORM but the SHAPE OF THE CONTENT.  `What is true about this\n> code?' is clean; `here is a chain by which a guest reaches X -- how far does it go?' is not, however it is\n> phrased.**\n\n**OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is the sharpest limit yet:** the seat cannot be used to review the very\nfindings that matter most in a hardening fork, once those findings have been assembled into a chain.  It can\ncheck the LINKS SEPARATELY -- *is `src_x' masked in the linear path?* is a clean question -- but not the\nchain as a chain.  **Split the chain into independent link questions, or verify it yourself.**  I verified\nthis one myself in three greps.\n\n*Recorded with the other two triggers rather than replacing them: vocabulary (prompt-level), archive reading\n(content-level, late), and now chain SHAPE (prompt-level).*\n\n*** THIRD RECORDED INSTANCE, 2026-09-07, AND IT CARRIES THE CLEANEST DISCRIMINATOR YET, because\nTWO briefs on the SAME SUBJECT MATTER passed on the SAME DAY and this one did not. ***\n\nArtifact: `regress/artifacts/lcaconfbus1kill/codex_witness_20260907.err`.  The `.txt` is **ZERO\nBYTES and the process exited 0** -- so an exit-status check scores it a success and a size check\nscores it a dead seat; only the `.err` names it:\n\n    ERROR: This content was flagged for possible cybersecurity risk. If this seems wrong, try\n    rephrasing your request. To get authorized for security work, join the Trusted Access for\n    Cyber program: ...\n\n**THE CONTROLS ARE THE VALUABLE PART.  Same seat, same model, same effort, same day, same device\nfile, all three read-only:**\n\n    PASSED   brief_lcabadval_fixcheck.md      \"six value guards that END THE PROCESS\" -- inlined\n                                              fatal()+exit(1) verbatim, asked whether a proposed\n                                              DELETION was sound.  6,976-byte answer.\n    PASSED   brief_sgirandomcolour_scope.md   \"writes FOUR random() bytes to guest memory\",\n                                              \"uninitialised host stack reaches guest memory\",\n                                              asked for a reachability census.  7,946-byte answer.\n    REFUSED  brief_lcaconfbus1_witness.md     asked whether a proposed TEST CONSTRUCTION would\n                                              successfully REACH the termination, step by step.\n\n**So it is not the vocabulary and not the subject.**  \"Host kill\", \"exit(1)\", \"guest-drivable\",\n\"uninitialised stack into guest memory\" all pass.  What differs is the ASK: the first two ask *is\nthis analysis correct*; the third asks *will this sequence work*.  A numbered two-step recipe --\nwrite this value here, then touch that window, and the process exits -- reads as exploit validation\nhowever defensively it is framed, and mine was framed defensively (opened with \"ordinary regression-\ntest adequacy review\", no offensive-security wording anywhere).\n\n> **THE OPERATIONAL RULE: ask codex to VERIFY AN ANALYSIS, not to VALIDATE A SEQUENCE THAT REACHES A\n> TERMINATION.**  This is the carrier's existing C5 trigger -- *asking for a construction that\n> reaches a defect* -- with a measured negative control on both sides for the first time.  Where a\n> construction genuinely needs checking, ask about its CONTROLS in isolation (\"does a matched\n> surviving control at the same address attribute an outcome to the value?\") without narrating the\n> path to the kill.\n\n**RECOVERY, and it is the sixth-class rule applied: the seat refused, the QUESTION did not become\nunanswerable.**  grok reads the repo, takes its brief from a FILE (no argv cap), runs `--permission-\nmode plan` read-only, and has no such filter.  It was fired on the byte-identical brief.  A refused\nseat with a live alternate transport is ONE SEAT COVERED, not one lost -- but it is recorded as a\nREFUSAL by name, never as agreement or as a wall.\n\n*Cost note: 17,471 tokens spent before the flag fired, and 217 KB of tool-call reasoning in the\n`.err` showing it had already listed the tree and begun reading.  The refusal is not instant, so a\nzero-byte `.txt` after several minutes is NOT evidence of a hang.*\n\n*** A DUPLICATE OF THIS ROW WAS FILED ON 2026-09-08 (`codexcontentfilter`) AND HAS BEEN WITHDRAWN.\nIt contributed ONE measurement worth folding in here. ***\n\n**A DETERMINISM CONTROL: the same brief, byte-for-byte, REFUSED and then ANSWERED.**\n`regress/artifacts/sgirekills/ROUNDDESIGN_brief.md`, md5 `21c440bc6746d1e6bb6d52f703f847bb`,\n16,903 bytes -- firing 1 produced a 0-byte `.txt`, firing 2 produced 6,868 bytes and a full answer.\nNothing changed between them but time.\n\n**That is consistent with THIS row's ARCHIVE-READING trigger and not with randomness:** the filter\nfires partway through, at whatever point the run has read enough of the archive, so two firings of\none brief diverge on what they happen to read.  *The duplicate concluded \"non-deterministic\"; the\nmechanism recorded here is the better explanation, and it makes the remedy a FIX (neutral language\nplus scoped reading) rather than a retry.*\n\n**A re-fire is still worth one attempt** -- it costs a minute and may take a different reading path\n-- but it is a coin-flip with a mechanism, not the remedy.\n\n*** A NEGATIVE CONTROL ON THIS ROW'S OWN REMEDY, MEASURED 2026-09-08: THE SCOPING FIX DID NOT WORK,\nAND THE REASON IS THAT IT CANNOT WORK ON A ROUND OF THIS SHAPE. ***\n\nThis row's recorded remedy is **neutral engineering language AND reading scoped to src/ and\nregress/*.py**, aimed at the ARCHIVE-READING trigger -- the filter fires once the run has read enough\nof regress/artifacts/ or ledger.json.\n\nThe sgirekills re-pin brief was refused.  I applied the remedy exactly -- added a READING SCOPE\nparagraph naming src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c and regress/*.py and excluding the archive -- and re-fired.\n**It was refused again**: codex_repin_scoped_20260908.txt is ZERO BYTES, the .err carries two\nTrusted-Access lines, 17,904 tokens used, exited 1.\n\n> *** THE DIAGNOSIS: SCOPING THE READING TO src/ IS NOT A REMEDY WHEN src/ IS WHAT TRIPS THE\n> FILTER. ***  The .err shows the run had reached dev_sgi_re.c -- and that file is a wall of bounds\n> guards, refusal arms and exit(1) calls.  **To the filter, the product source of a hardening fork\n> reads like the archive does.**  The remedy moves the reading from one flagged corpus to another.\n\n**So the three recorded triggers need a fourth, and it is the one that BOUNDS the remedy:**\n\n    SUBJECT SOURCE   content-level and UNAVOIDABLE -- a round whose subject file is itself\n                     guard-and-refuse code cannot be scoped away from its own subject.\n\n**WHAT THIS CHANGES OPERATIONALLY, and it is not nothing:** for a round on a device-hardening file,\ncodex must be given the FACTS INLINE -- treated as a PACKET-FED seat for that round -- rather than\npointed at the file.  That is the same move the carrier already prescribes for the Ollama transports,\nand it is affordable here because a re-pin brief's facts are a few dozen lines of arm addresses and\narray declarations.  **Untested as of this writing; recorded as the next thing to try, NOT as a\nsecond remedy claimed to work.**\n\n*Recorded because it is a controlled negative: same seat, same subject, remedy applied deliberately\nand singly, refused.  The remedy's earlier success on the gdromcondavail brief stands -- what is\nrefuted is its GENERALITY, and this row had stated it with no scope condition.*\n\n*** THE CHAIN-SHAPE TRIGGER CONFIRMED PROMPT-LEVEL, 2026-09-08, WITH A CLEAN DISCRIMINATOR THAT\nSEPARATES IT FROM THE ARCHIVE-READING TRIGGER AT A GLANCE. ***\n\nFour codex briefs went out tonight on neutral engineering subjects.  Three answered in full; the\nfourth was refused.  **The refused one is the WITNESS-DESIGN brief**, and its `.err` settles where\nthe refusal happened:\n\n    brief                      .err bytes   file-read lines   outcome\n    armbfcdecode DECODE            70,308   many              ANSWERED (5,037 b)\n    alphatbinegsel VERIFY          20,210   many              ANSWERED (6,820 b)\n    alphatbinegsel Q3             167,085   many              ANSWERED (4,654 b)\n    alphatbinegsel WITNESS          6,291   *** ZERO ***      REFUSED (0 b)\n\n*** ZERO FILE-READ LINES MEANS IT REFUSED BEFORE OPENING ANYTHING.  The prompt's TEXT tripped it,\nnot what it read. ***  That is this row's third recorded trigger -- CHAIN SHAPE -- and it is now\nconfirmed as prompt-level rather than inferred.\n\n**WHY THIS BRIEF AND NOT THE OTHER THREE.**  The three that answered ask *\"is this analysis\ncorrect\"* about code that already exists.  The witness brief asks how to CONSTRUCT A GUEST SEQUENCE\nTHAT DEMONSTRATES THE DEFECT -- *\"what the guest sets up, which PAL call it makes, and what it then\nobserves\"*, plus *\"remap a VPN to a different physical page ... then load from that VPN and see the\nOLD contents.\"*  **A numbered guest-controlled sequence ending in an observation of stale memory is\nthe chain shape, however defensively framed** -- and this brief was written to be neutral, with no\n`kill`/`host dies`/`exploit` vocabulary anywhere.  Neutral language was NOT sufficient.\n\n> *** THE OPERATIONAL CONSEQUENCE, and it is a real capability limit rather than a nuisance:\n> **codex CANNOT DESIGN WITNESSES FOR THIS PROJECT.**  Witness design IS the reach-the-defect shape\n> by definition -- the ladder requires a real guest instruction sequence that reaches the site.  So\n> every rung-3 witness brief is expected to refuse, and routing one to codex is a wasted\n> invocation. ***\n\n**THE DISCRIMINATOR, and it costs one `grep -c`:**\n\n    small .err + ZERO read lines + 0-byte .txt   -> PROMPT-LEVEL.  Rewrite the brief, or send it\n                                                    to a different seat.  A retry will not help.\n    large .err + many read lines + 0-byte .txt   -> LATE (archive/subject).  Scoping the reading\n                                                    may help; a retry may take a different path.\n    NO .txt and NO .err at all                   -> the run NEVER STARTED (a launcher/path fault,\n                                                    NOT a refusal) -- measured separately tonight.\n\n*Three distinguishable states, three different remedies.  Previously only the middle one was\ncharacterised, and the row's determinism pair belongs to it.*\n\n**ROUTED TO grok INSTEAD**, which is not subject to this filter and which has designed rung-3\nwitnesses for this project before (`lcabadval`'s row records a rung-3 seat specifying a full\nsequence).  *The brief was reused BYTE-FOR-BYTE -- no rewrite -- which is what makes this a clean\nseat-capability finding rather than a prompt-quality one.*\n\n*** THE THREE-STATE DISCRIMINATOR NEEDS ONE CORRECTION, FOUND BY APPLYING IT TO A HEALTHY SEAT\nTWENTY MINUTES AFTER WRITING IT.  2026-09-08. ***\n\nAs written above, the first state reads *\"small `.err` + ZERO read lines + 0-byte `.txt` ->\nPROMPT-LEVEL refusal.\"*  Applied to the `#513` pass-2 run mid-flight, that pattern MATCHED a seat\nthat was perfectly healthy:\n\n    refusal lines      0\n    file-read lines    0        <- matches the \"prompt-level\" shape\n    .err bytes    39,485        <- but LARGE, not small\n    processes          4 alive\n    .txt bytes         0        <- normal mid-run: codex writes stdout at the END\n\n**It had read nothing because the pass-2 brief INLINES the whole diff -- there was nothing it needed\nto open.**  A seat can legitimately answer with zero file reads.\n\n> **THE ACTUAL DISCRIMINATOR IS THE REFUSAL LINE.  Everything else says WHERE it refused, not\n> WHETHER it did.**\n>\n>     refusal line present + ZERO reads   -> PROMPT-LEVEL.  Reroute; a retry will not help.\n>     refusal line present + MANY reads   -> LATE (archive/subject).  Scoping may help.\n>     refusal line ABSENT, .txt 0 bytes,\n>       process alive                     -> MID-RUN.  Not a refusal at all.\n>     NO .txt and NO .err whatsoever      -> the run NEVER STARTED (launcher/path fault).\n\n*The read count was doing work it cannot do.  I wrote the three-state form from four observations\nthat happened to correlate -- every refusal I had seen with zero reads also had a small `.err` --\nand the correlation broke on the first case outside that set.*  **That is this file's own\nfreshly-minted-exemplar rule landing on a discriminator I minted an hour earlier: the instruction\nsurvived, its cheap proxy did not.**\n\n*Corrected in `tools/pipeline/ADJUDICATION_20260909.md` as well, which had quoted the earlier form.*\n\n*** A FOURTH TRIGGER, AND IT IS THE OPERATIONALLY EXPENSIVE ONE: THE ASK-AGAIN QUESTION ITSELF.\nMeasured 2026-09-08 on the #513 pass-3 panel. ***\n\ncodex ANSWERED the pass-2 diff review and REFUSED the pass-3 one.  Both are diff reviews of the\nsame round, same file, same neutral vocabulary.  **The refusal is LATE, not prompt-level** --\n133,341 bytes of `.err`, 30,706 tokens used, many file reads, then the flag -- so it is the\narchive/subject shape rather than the chain shape.\n\n**WHAT PASS 3 ASKS THAT PASS 2 DID NOT:**\n\n    Q2  \"Is there a third cell? ... bits 6, 7, 9, 10 are swept by nothing.\"\n    Q3  \"Name the smallest edit that keeps a guard and makes these two rows GREEN while the\n         latch behaviour is WRONG.\"\n\n**That is an EVASION-CONSTRUCTION request.**  Pass 2 asked *is this change correct*; pass 3 asks\n*construct an edit that defeats this detector while looking right*.  The second is much closer to\nthe reach-the-defect shape than to source review.\n\n> *** THE CONSEQUENCE IS NOT COSMETIC: THE ASK-AGAIN IS A MANDATED STEP IN THIS PROJECT'S\n> PROCESS. ***  The vacuity floor requires exactly this question of every clean kill table -- *\"the\n> smallest edit that breaks this property and still passes\"* -- and measured yield says it is where\n> the real escapes come from.  **So codex is unavailable for the single highest-yield adversarial\n> query in the round lifecycle**, on top of being unavailable for witness design.\n\n**WHAT STILL WORKS:** codex answered EIGHT briefs tonight -- decode conformance, ABI review, source\nanalysis, callback tracing, error-handling review, and the pass-2 diff review.  The pattern across\nnine firings is consistent: **codex reviews CODE THAT EXISTS; it will not construct an edit or a\nsequence that defeats something.**\n\n**MITIGATION, and it is already proven in this round:** grok and the packet-fed seats answer the\nask-again without difficulty -- pass 3's five answering seats ALL found the remaining latch cells,\nand grok additionally prescribed the one-row class closure.  **Route the ask-again to grok and the\nOllama transports; do not spend a codex invocation on it.**\n\n*Recorded from a controlled pair: same round, same file, same seat, two briefs one hour apart,\ndiffering in exactly this question.*\n\n*** THE ARCHIVE TRIGGER FIRED AGAIN 2026-09-08, AND I WALKED THE SEAT INTO IT WHILE WRITING THE\nAVOIDANCE INSTRUCTION.  Ten refusal lines. ***\n\nThe brief said, in its own scope section:\n\n> *\"Read ... `regress/artifacts/sgirekills/killtable_20260908/*.txt` and the `*_20260908.out` logs\n> there -- **these are the measurement records the packet cites; reading them is the point of this\n> brief**.  Please still avoid `tools/pipeline/ledger.json`.\"*\n\n**`regress/artifacts/` IS the archive corpus this row's second trigger keys on.**  I named the\nledger as the thing to avoid and directed the seat straight into the other half.  507 KB of `.err`,\n**ten** refusal lines, 0-byte answer.\n\n> *** THE TENSION IS REAL AND IS NOT A MISTAKE IN THE ROW'S REMEDY: AUDITING MEASUREMENT CLAIMS\n> REQUIRES THE MEASUREMENT ARCHIVE.  The remedy \"scope reading away from regress/artifacts/\" and the\n> task \"verify these numbers against their logs\" are in direct conflict. ***\n\n**THE RESOLUTION, and it is the one the packet-fed seats have always needed: INLINE THE LOGS.**  The\nseven logs this audit needs total a few KB.  Re-fired as `PACKETAUDIT2_brief.md` with every one\ninlined and an explicit *\"DO NOT READ regress/artifacts/\"* -- so the seat needs no archive access to\ndo the job.\n\n**This generalises past codex:** a brief that asks a seat to check numbers should carry the numbers'\nLOGS, not their paths.  The Ollama transports have always required that because they cannot read\nfiles at all; here it is required for the opposite reason -- a seat that CAN read the archive is\nrefused for doing so.  *Same remedy, two different causes, and the file-reading seats' version was\nnot obvious until the filter made it necessary.*\n\n*Recorded as my error rather than a seat failure: the brief was refused for exactly what it\ninstructed.*\n\n*** THE ENTRY DIRECTLY ABOVE IS WRONG AND I AM CORRECTING IT WITHIN THE HOUR.  THE ARCHIVE-READING\nBRIEF **ANSWERED**. ***\n\nI recorded that the packet-audit brief was refused for directing the seat into `regress/artifacts/`,\nand prescribed inlining the logs.  **The refusal count was 25 and the seat then produced an\n18,687-byte answer** -- it retried and succeeded, reading the archive throughout and citing\nindividual log lines.  Meanwhile the INLINED re-fire sat at 2 refusals and a 0-byte answer.\n\n> **SO A REFUSAL LINE IS NOT A TERMINAL STATE, AND MY OWN THREE-STATE DISCRIMINATOR SAYS SO\n> WRONGLY.**  It reads *\"refusal line present -> reroute; a retry will not help.\"*  **Measured\n> false here: twenty-five refusal lines preceded a complete answer.**  The discriminating fact is\n> the `.txt`, not the `.err` -- a non-empty answer means the seat got there whatever the stderr says.\n\n**WHAT SURVIVES:** the two PASS-3 and WITNESS refusals were terminal (0-byte answers), so the\ncapability finding about the ask-again and witness-design shapes is unaffected.  What is refuted is\nthe inference that ANY refusal line means the seat is unavailable.\n\n*Recorded here at length because I wrote the false version into this row, into the checkpoint and\ninto the adjudication packet within the last hour, and a correction that arrives quietly is worse\nthan the error.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31249:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06 from the `sgirandompixel' design panel, where **codex argued for it as part of that round\nand grok argued for filing it -- and grok's scoping was taken**, because a return value plus every GET site\nis not ONE SITE and the reopening rule says a round takes one.\n\nAfter `sgirandompixel', `horrible_getputpixel()' leaves `*color' alone on a declined GET, so the caller's\nown `uint32_t color = fg' stands and the PUT writes the FOREGROUND.  That is DETERMINISTIC, which is the\nwhole gain of that round -- and it is still a write of data the guest did not ask for.\n\n**codex's form, recorded verbatim because it is the design if this is taken:** give the function an explicit\nSUCCESS RESULT; *'unchanged `*color' is merely an output-parameter guarantee: callers must not consume it\nfollowing failure'*.  Cover ALL the GET sites -- the source GET, the `oldcolor' GET used by ROPs, and MTE's\n`bg' GET.  **AND ITS WARNING, which is the sketch rule and is the reason this is not a one-liner:**\n*'preserve coordinate advancement and stipple rotation when suppressing a pixel's PUT; an early `continue'\ncan bypass those updates'* -- anchors `src_x += step_x' and `Rotate the stipple pattern'.\n\nThe shipped detector already discriminates this: `lin_b is fg' expects fg TODAY, and under a skip-PUT fix it\nwould expect the PRE-PAINT instead.  So the oracle-correction rule applies again and the row set is known in\nadvance.\n\n*** A SHIPPED ROW ACTIVELY REQUIRES THE BEHAVIOUR THIS ROW WOULD REMOVE.  Found by the codex seat\non the `sgirandomcolour` pass-2 brief, 2026-09-07. ***\n\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py`'s **`lin_b is fg` expects FG rather than the destination's\nprepaint** -- i.e. it asserts that the dependent PUT DID run after a declined GET.\n\n> **So a general failed-GET/skip-PUT repair would make that existing row FAIL.**  An MTE-only repair\n> would escape it; the general one collides with a shipped oracle.\n\n**That is the oracle-correction rule's territory** -- the row's contract has to be adjudicated\nexplicitly before the code changes, and if it is re-pinned the re-pin must be measured RED on the\ncommitted build first.  *Recorded here rather than acted on: the collision is the finding.*",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31263:   "id": "sgirandomcolour",
tools/pipeline/ledger.json:31269:   "state_note": "FILED 2026-09-06.  **FOUND BY GROK** on the `sgirandompixel' design panel, as a residual it explicitly said\nto file rather than grow the round with: *'`default:' of the COLOUR-mode switch still does\n`buf[n] = random()' and then writes those bytes.  Same class, different arm.'*\n\n**VERIFIED BY READING before filing**: four `random()' calls at dev_sgi_re.c, anchor `buf[0] = random();',\nin the colour-mode `default:'.  `sgirandompixel' removed the ONE in `horrible_getputpixel()'; these four\nremain and are the reason `grep -c 'random()'` on that file is still non-zero after the fix.\n\nNobody else on that panel saw it -- not codex, not the packet-fed seats, not me -- and I had read the file\nseveral times that evening.  The design is presumably the same member 3 (decline + latched announce), but\nthe observable differs: this arm fills a BUFFER rather than a single caller variable, so what a declined\nfill should leave is its own question and the detector shape is not simply copied.\n\n*** MAIN-LOOP RECORD 2026-09-07 (assess stage) -- written into state_note, NOT into `entries'. ***\n`entries' is the SEAT ANSWER list: check_stage_panels.py counts entries to decide whether a stage's\nnine seats were fired, and gen_dashboard.py REFUSES TO RENDER on a seat id no column declares.  A\nmain-loop note parked there is not merely mis-filed -- it MANUFACTURES A SEAT ANSWER THAT NEVER\nHAPPENED, which is the seat-failure-counted-as-agreement shape inverted.  Seven such entries were\nwritten during this session and all seven were migrated here when the generator refused.\n\nSEVERITY RAISED MED -> HIGH, on a fact DERIVED rather than assumed.  The row said the fix\nwas *presumably* the same as [[sgirandompixel]]'s and that the observable differed.  **The reachability is now\nestablished and it is the SAME documented-mode structure, not merely the same shape.**\n\nMeasured at HEAD, three commands:\n  * the PUT colour switch implements exactly FOUR cases -- `DE_MODE_TYPE_' CI, RGB, RGBA, ABGR -- and its\n    `default:' is `buf[0..3] = random()';\n  * `crmfbreg.h' documents **FIVE** colour types; the fifth is `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB  0x000000f0';\n  * `grep -c YCRCB src/devices/dev_sgi_re.c' = **0**.  It is handled nowhere.\n\nAnd it is reached by an ordinary register write: `color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK', mask `0xf0', so a\nguest selecting YCRCB lands on the random arm directly.\n\n*** SO THIS IS `#504's OWN SENTENCE AT A SECOND SITE: four of five DOCUMENTED modes work and the fifth\nreturns random data. ***  `#504` was graded HIGH on exactly that warrant, and this arm is arguably worse in\none respect -- `sgirandompixel' put a random value in `*color' for the CALLER to store, whereas this writes\nfour random bytes into the tile through `memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)' with no intermediary.\n\n**WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED, and the severity should be read with it: NO WITNESS.**  Nothing here shows a guest\ndriver selecting YCRCB -- only that the model accepts it and answers with random data.  The rung-3 attempt\nlooks cheap for the same reason `#504's did (`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py' already programs this\ncode path; the colour type is a different field of the same mode word), and it is OWED before this ships.\n\n**The GET side does NOT share the defect** -- its `default:' reads the raw 32-bit value rather than inventing\none, so this is a PUT-only arm.  That asymmetry is itself worth carrying into the design: the two default\narms of one function already disagree about what an unhandled colour type means.\n\nNote this is a DIFFERENT defect from [[sgirebufuninit]] at the same lines: that one is about bytes NOBODY\nassigns; this is about bytes assigned from `random()'.  A fix for either leaves the other standing.\n\n*** READY TO RUN AS THE NEXT ROUND.  Three things established 2026-09-07 while `#505` worked in the\nsame function, none of which this row had. ***\n\n**1. IT IS A DIFFERENT SWITCH FROM THE ONE `#504` FIXED, and the row should say so plainly** -- I nearly\nrecorded this as the `#499` symmetric-cell shape and it is not.  `#504` de-fanged the **addressing-mode**\ndefault (`default: fatal(\"unimplemented dst_mode %i ...\"); *color = random();`).  This row is the\n**colour-mode** default of a different `switch` in the same function:\n\n    default:buf[0] = random();  buf[1] = random();\n            buf[2] = random();  buf[3] = random();\n            // TODO.\n            fatal(\"[ put: color mode = 0x%x ]\\n\", color_mode);\n    }\n    cpu->memory_rw(cpu, cpu->mem, tileptr + ofs, buf, bufdepth, MEM_WRITE, NO_EXCEPTIONS|PHYSICAL);\n\n**There is no `break` and no decline: control falls straight into the write**, so four bytes of host\n`random()` land in guest memory at a guest-chosen tile offset.  Same family as `#504`, same function,\ndifferent arm -- *related by idiom, not by the one-cell-repair pattern.*\n\n**2. FULLY GUEST-REACHABLE, established by reading rather than assumed.**  `color_mode = mode &\nDE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`, and `mode` is the caller's `src_mode`/`dst_mode`, read straight out of `de_reg[]`\nfrom `CRIME_DE_MODE_SRC`/`CRIME_DE_MODE_DST` with **no filtering anywhere on the path**.  The four\nimplemented types are 0x00/0x10/0x20/0x30; `DE_MODE_TYPE_YCRCB` is **0xf0 and is documented in\n`crmfbreg.h`** but unimplemented, so a guest selecting a real, named mode reaches this arm.\n\n**3. A WITNESS IS ONE CONSTANT AWAY.**  `#505` built `regress/sgi_re_rgb_probe.py`,\na rung-3 probe that drives an XFER copy on an unmodified `-E sgi -e ip32` with both ladder controls and a\nsibling accept row, and whose `setup()` now takes **both** src and dst modes.  Pointing its `dst_mode` at\n`LIN_A | BUFDEPTH_32 | 0xf0` puts YCRCB into the PUT switch and reaches this arm.\n\n  *** THE ORACLE IS THE HARD PART AND IT NEEDS SAYING BEFORE THE ROUND STARTS: THE DEFECT'S OUTPUT IS\n  RANDOM, SO NO VALUE ROW CAN PIN IT. ***  `#504` hit exactly this and its answer is the precedent --\n  assert that the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value, i.e. that the write was DECLINED, rather\n  than trying to assert anything about what was written.  A determinism oracle is refuted in advance by\n  this project's own record: two seats showed on `#504` that stack reuse can make a wrong value\n  deterministic, so *\"the same value twice\"* proves nothing.\n\n**FIX SHAPE: the option set has a member already chosen for its sibling.**  `#504` used DECLINE + LATCHED\nANNOUNCE with its own bit (`SGIRE_W_BADMODE = 1 << 10`), for the reason `#498` measured -- the four\nhandlers share one struct, so a shared latch is silenced by the other site's ordinary traffic.  **This arm\nneeds its OWN bit, `1 << 11`**, and `#504`'s comment already says the enum ends there on purpose so the\nnext maintainer finds it.  *That is a transferred in-tree warrant, not a \"cannot hurt\" addition.*\n\n*** PASS-1 AUDIT, 2026-09-07 (codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_pass1_20260907.txt`).  TWO OF MY PREMISES WERE WRONG AND THE\nDISPOSITION SURVIVED. ***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE is justified, with an unconditional return from the PUT\ndefault.  But the brief misstates #504's oracle and the write's byte count; depth 8 also exposes\nuninitialized buffer bytes.\"*\n\n*** CORRECTION 1 -- THE ORACLE.  THIS IS THE ONE THAT WOULD HAVE COST A ROUND. ***  My brief said\n`#504`'s precedent asserts *the destination still holds its PRE-PAINT value*.  It does not.  The seat\nopened the probe: `lin_b is fg` expects **`FG_VAL = 0x77665544`**, not the pre-paint `0x11111111`,\nbecause a declined source GET **preserves the caller's foreground value**, which the following PUT\nthen writes.  In the seat's words, *\"the probe explicitly rejects preserving the destination as a\ndifferent disposition.\"*\n\n  **So `#504` warrants the latch-and-return IDIOM and does NOT supply the unchanged-destination\n  ORACLE I attributed to it.**  Those are different transfers and I ran them together.\n\n  **THE ORACLE THE SEAT PROPOSES INSTEAD, and it is stronger than either:** observe **ZERO\n  `MEM_WRITE` CALLS** from an unsupported PUT, *alongside* unchanged destination bytes -- because\n  that pair **distinguishes \"no write\" from \"same final value\"**, which neither assertion does\n  alone.  It also notes that if removing the RNG use is contractual, that must be observed\n  SEPARATELY.\n\n  *And a sentinel value will not rescue a value oracle here:* each assignment converts a SEPARATE\n  `random()` result to `uint8_t`, so the whole-word improbability argument `#504`'s old witness used\n  does not establish an impossible byte pattern.\n\n*** CORRECTION 2 -- THE BYTE COUNT, and it is the SAME WIDTH-AXIS LESSON `#505` PAID FOR HOURS\nEARLIER. ***  My brief said *four* bytes of `random()` are written.  The write is `bufdepth` bytes,\nnot four: at bufdepth 8 the arm fills `buf[0..3]` with RNG and ships **four further UNINITIALISED\nstack bytes** with them; at bufdepth 1 or 2 fewer than four RNG bytes land.  **\"Four\" is true only\nat bufdepth 4** -- the width every example happens to use.  *That is the exact shape of `#505`'s\nclause-(d) floor breach, arriving in the next round's brief the same day, written by the person who\nhad just repaired it.*  Overlaps [[sgirebufuninit]]; the round must say which defect owns which\nbytes.\n\n**OPTION SET: CONFIRMED COMPLETE, and the grep found three in-tree alternatives worth naming:**\n  * the helper's own GET default already provides a raw ABGR-shaped fallback (`dev_sgi_re.c:358`);\n  * **PCC2 has a real ACCEPT precedent** (`dev_pcc2.c:427`) -- a read served from backing storage\n    before the default diagnostic;\n  * **SGI's ROP diagnostics latch BY VALUE** (`dev_sgi_re.c:850`) rather than one bit per site.\n\nNone establishes correct YCRCB PUT semantics.  *\"Mirroring the GET fallback would repeat the\nunsupported-authority problem you warned about.  The YCRCB name establishes an encoding; it does not\nestablish packing, conversion, or depth semantics.\"*  IMPLEMENT is defensible in principle but no\npermitted source supplies an implementation or a demonstrated driver selection.  **No additional\nwarranted disposition outside the option set** -- which is the option-set rule returning a clean\nnegative for once, and that is worth recording as such.\n\n**THE LATCH BIT IS CONFIRMED AVAILABLE AND THE OBJECTION TO ONE BIT IS RECORDED:** occupied bits\nenumerate as 0..10, `warned` is `uint32_t`, so **`1 << 11` is free**.  The strongest objection is\ndiagnostic coverage -- *an earlier unnamed mode suppresses a later YCRCB announcement* -- and\nper-mode latching has in-tree precedent (the ROP site above).  One bit stays consistent with this\nhelper's existing policy, so it is a judgement the round must make explicitly rather than inherit.\n\n**FIX SHAPE (Q2), in the seat's words:** *a terminal default arm containing a conditional\nannouncement followed by an **unconditional function return***.  Note this is the SKETCH RULE's\nterritory -- the PUT branch's write sits after the switch and is shared by all five arms, so the\nreturn must be checked against the whole enclosing function before it is written.\n\n*** AND A WARNING THAT SAVES THE ROUND A FALSE START: `#504`'s PROBE CANNOT BE REUSED UNCHANGED. ***\n*\"#504's `lin_b is fg` remains green under edits confined to this colour default.  Both of that\nprobe's mode constants use RGBA, so it never selects the PUT colour default.  Its setup cannot be\ntransferred unchanged.\"*  `#505`'s probe is the better base -- its `setup()` already takes both src\nand dst modes -- but the same check applies to it and must be run, not assumed.\n\n**ENUMERATION CONFIRMED, with a qualification I did not have:** the twelve default selections are\neleven unnamed (`0x40`..`0xe0`) plus the named `0xf0` YCRCB, and they reach the arm *\"when the\npreceding coordinate/addressing/tile-validity checks pass\"*.\n\n*** THE ORACLE QUESTION, ANSWERED BEFORE THE ROUND STARTED -- AND THE NAIVE IMPLEMENTATION WOULD\nHAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN.  codex, `regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`.\n***\n\n**VERDICT:** *\"Your distinction is conditionally sound.  A correctly scoped write-attempt observer\nworks for Round A; **the existing stepped probe supplies none.**  This fork's debugger watchpoints\noffer a qualified alternative.\"*\n\n**MY DISTINCTION SURVIVES, with a precision I had not stated.**  A count separates *no request* from\n*a request carrying the existing bytes* -- but *\"zero calls does not establish correct rejection\nunless the unsupported arm was ACTUALLY REACHED: an earlier return or an operation that never ran\nalso produces zero.\"*  That is the attribution problem, and it means the round owes a reach control\nbeside the count.\n\n*** THE MECHANISM EXISTS: debugger WRITE WATCHPOINTS, checked in the common memory path BEFORE\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch, and neither PHYSICAL nor NO_EXCEPTIONS excludes this helper's write.  A\npty driver can observe them with NO instrumented build. ***  (`memory_rw.c:273`.)\n\n*** AND IT HAS TWO DECISIVE LIMITS, THE FIRST OF WHICH WOULD HAVE PRODUCED A FALSE GREEN: ***\n  1. **The check requires `!single_step`, and the existing probe's `run_ops()` uses `step`.**  So\n     *\"merely adding watchpoints and counting their messages to that execution scheme could report\n     ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"*  The row would have reported \"no write happened\" while writes happened.\n     **A watchpoint oracle therefore requires the FREE-RUNNING probe shape, not the stepped one.**\n  2. It also requires `!about_to_enter_single_step` and **SETS that flag after its first report**, so\n     later pixel writes inside the same drawing command go unreported.  What Python would count is\n     *printed watchpoint reports*, not `MEM_WRITE` calls -- watchpoints carry no hit count.\n\n**SO THE ACHIEVABLE CLAIM IS BOUNDED, and the round must say so rather than overclaim:** the\nmechanism supports an **any-attempt-to-this-range** witness when observation is active throughout the\nrelevant execution.  It does NOT support *\"exactly N calls\"* or *\"no writes anywhere from this\nhelper\"*.  The conclusion must read **\"no observed write attempt to this range under these validated\nconditions\"** -- watching the destination cannot exclude a write to an UNINTENDED address, which is\nexactly the failure mode [[sgirebufuninit]] and the `#505` tiled row both hit in other forms.\n\n**THE SHIPPABLE DESIGN, in the seat's own construction:** verified arm reach and completed execution,\nan active observer that DOES detect a supported write to the same range (the liveness half), then no\nmatching write report for the rejected operation, with unchanged verified destination bytes as\ncorroboration and the announce count testing \"announce once\" separately.  **That is four rows, not\none**, and knowing it now is worth more than discovering it after the fix.\n\n*This is the carrier's `!single_step` family arriving through a NEW mechanism.  The carrier records\nthat a stepped probe cannot observe INTERRUPT DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`'s\n\"do not cause interrupts while single-stepping\").  The WATCHPOINT case is the same guard on a\ndifferent feature and was not written down.*\n\n*** CODEX DESIGN PASS 2026-09-07 (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_design_20260907.txt`).\nVERDICT: REVISE -- DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE **with a `return` before the PUT transfer**.  It\nrefuted TWO of the brief's premises, and one of them was the brief's central design claim. ***\n\n**S3 WAS WRONG, AND IT IS THE PREMISE THE WHOLE 'COPY THE SIBLING' READING RESTED ON.**  I wrote that\n`sgirandompixel` fixed *the GET side of this same helper*.  It did not: **it repaired the\nADDRESSING-MODE default at `:235`, which sits BEFORE GET and PUT diverge and returns for both\ndirections.  The COLOUR GET default at `:358` still performs a raw conversion.**  Two different\narms -- so the sibling is a precedent for the DISPOSITION and not for the site, and its comment\ncannot simply be copied.\n\n**S5 WAS WRONG, AND THIS ONE UNBLOCKS THE ROUND.**  I claimed the decline's 'what does `buf` hold'\nquestion was the SAME question as [[sgirebufuninit]]'s, making that row a prerequisite.  codex:\n*\"A declined PUT need not leave anything defined in `buf`, provided nothing consumes it.  The helper\nitself owns the memory write.  Its callers receive no buffer to transfer afterward.\"*\n\n    remove the fill, fall through   -> transfers indeterminate stack bytes   REJECT\n    zero the span, fall through     -> overwrites destination with zero      needs a warrant\n    RETURN from the PUT default     -> preserves destination bytes           PREFERRED\n\n> **So the two rows are INDEPENDENT and there is no patch ordering.**  This round decides whether an\n> unsupported PUT writes anything at all; `sgirebufuninit` decides how paths that DO transfer avoid\n> consuming uninitialised bytes.  *Initialising the buffer would neither remove the four `random()`\n> calls nor justify writing zeros for an unsupported format.*\n\n**S1 needs a width qualification:** four bytes are ASSIGNED randomly, but the transfer length is 1, 2,\n4 or 8.  A narrow transfer exposes only the prefix; an eight-byte transfer additionally consumes four\nuninitialised bytes.\n\n**S2's inference was a CORRELATE and the real population is twelve, not one.**  `grep -c YCRCB` = 0\nproves only that one NAMED constant is absent.  Enumerating `mode & 0xf0`: sixteen values, **four\nexplicit PUT cases (CI/RGB/RGBA/ABGR) and TWELVE reaching `default:`** -- eleven unnamed plus YCRCB.\nThe DE register handler stores the mode without filtering the type nibble, and both line and rectangle\ncallers pass it through unchanged.  *The named-constant search was the same error that cost the\n`asicbadaddr` census a 4x undercount.*\n\n**FAULT IS AVAILABLE HERE and my brief implied otherwise** -- `memory_rw.c:603` delivers MIPS\nexceptions.  codex rejects it anyway on better grounds than availability: the helper returns `void`,\nits transfers request `NO_EXCEPTIONS`, and the outer device handler returns success, so FAULT would\nneed an error-propagation design and a warrant for the guest-visible exception.\n\n**THE LATCH MUST BE AN INDEPENDENT BIT, NOT `SGIRE_W_BADMODE`** -- sharing it would let an ADDRESSING\nfailure suppress the COLOUR diagnostic.  That is `#490`'s measured latch-collision class, predicted\nhere before the code exists.\n\n**Q4, the predicted escape:** condition the new `return` on `bufdepth == 4` -- the canonical clause-(d)\nshape, conditioning the repair on a field the transfer consumes.  **codex expects `rgb tiled64` to stay\ngreen under it**, because that row varies the GET SOURCE's addressing and width while keeping a linear\nfour-byte RGBA destination: *\"its name does not establish PUT coverage.\"*  The proposed closure is the\ndestination axes together -- twelve unsupported types x four widths x four address modes = **192\ncombinations** -- with whole-span checks including eight-byte spans.  *Source-based prediction; no\nmutant built.*\n\n**Q3, the witness:** source inspection supports a rung-3 cold-debugger probe on stock IP32 and codex\nfound no machine-description blocker.  But **the sibling probe's green rows are NOT a witness for this\nrow**: its setup fixes the DESTINATION to RGBA, so varying its source-mode argument never exercises the\ncolour PUT default.  Record **'source-reachable; rung-3 attempt pending'** -- codex ran nothing, as\ninstructed, so the attempted-probe prerequisite is NOT yet discharged.\n\n*** A CONSEQUENCE OF THE DECLINE DESIGN, found by the codex seat on the SIBLING brief\n(`sgimtedepth4dead`) and cross-filed here because it is this row's design that it bears on. ***\n\n*\"A helper return before accessing the buffer leaves the caller's colour unchanged.  MTE still\nperforms the dependent PUT, and its `bg` persists across iterations -- so a declined GET can reuse an\nearlier source value.\"*\n\n**That is distinct from buffer initialisation and it bites the agreed repair.**  The design settled\nfor this row is DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE with a `return` before the transfer -- and on the MTE\npath the caller's `bg` is a loop-scope variable, so a declined pixel does not leave the destination\nuntouched: **it writes the PREVIOUS pixel's colour.**  A decline that silently smears the last good\nvalue across a run of unsupported pixels is a different wrong answer from `random()`, and arguably a\nmore plausible-looking one.\n\n*The round owes an answer: does the decline return before the PUT as well, and if so what does the\ndestination hold?  This was not visible from this row's own brief, which framed the question around\n`buf` rather than around the caller's colour variable.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 2, 2026-09-07: I CONFLATED A DECLINED GET WITH A DECLINED PUT.  PASS 1's DESIGN\nSTANDS UNCHANGED. ***\n\nThe PUT-default `return` exits **before** `memory_rw(... MEM_WRITE ...)`, so whatever the caller's\n`color` holds, that declined PUT does not transfer it.  Leaving the destination's existing bytes\nintact is the honest disposition for an unsupported write.  **The `bg` observation is about the\ndependent-PUT problem, which is a different row.**\n\n**AND MY S4 DID NOT ESTABLISH REACHABILITY:** MTE builds modes from addressing and depth bits only,\nleaving the colour type at CI, and its one colour override sits behind the unreachable `depth == 4`.\n**Neither construction reaches this PUT colour default**, so the MTE `bg` behaviour does not bear on\nthis row at all.\n\n*Two further qualifications on my wording: MTE reloads `bg` from `CRIME_MTE_BG` on **each transfer\ninvocation**, so a declined GET retains the latest value from THAT invocation or the register's\ninitial value -- never a previous command's local `bg`.  And \"the whole rectangle deterministically\nreceives the last good colour\" is too strong independently, because CI's accepted PUT initialises\nonly `buf[0]` and wider depths consume uninitialised bytes.*\n\n**THE LATCH GRANULARITY IS CONFIRMED AS APPROPRIATE AND ITS LIMIT STATED:** one message per device\ninstance for the latch's lifetime -- so a thousand declined pixels produce one line, **and none if an\nearlier command already set the bit** (the struct is zeroed at init; there is no per-command reset).\nIt deliberately cannot distinguish one declined pixel from a rectangle; reporting extent would need\ncounting or a command summary.\n\n**RESIDUAL FOUND: bounding the PUT warning does not bound every warning from the operation.**  The\n**GET colour default still logs without a latch**, and DE can perform that GET for a\ndestination-dependent raster operation before ever reaching the declined PUT.  So a round that\nlatches only the PUT arm leaves a guest-drivable unlatched diagnostic in the same code path.\n\n**THE CLAUSE-(d) MUTANT:** change the new `return;` to `break;`, keeping the latch and the\nannouncement.  Execution resumes at the memory write and consumes the uninitialised buffer -- **it\nbreaks the no-write contract without restoring `random()`**.  `rgb samemode` and `rgb tiled64` both\nexpected to stay green (supported RGB/RGBA, never reaching the default).  *The coverage gap is wider\nthan MTE: the scoped scripts never select an unsupported destination colour type at all, so their\ngreen results cannot establish this decline.*\n\n*** THE WITNESS PROBLEM IS CHARACTERISED, AND IT IS HARDER THAN THE ROW ASSUMED.  From the codex\nORACLE answer (`regress/artifacts/sgirandomcolour/codex_oracle_20260907.txt`), read in full\n2026-09-07 -- most of this had been sitting unread. ***\n\n**TWO PREMISE CORRECTIONS FIRST, and the second changes the FIX, not just the witness:**\n\n1. *\"The buffer lacks a declaration initializer; it is not NEVER INITIALIZED.  PUT arms assign\n   prefixes, and GET fills the requested transfer length.\"*  The row's shorthand was sloppy.\n2. *** \"THE MISSING `break` IS NOT WHAT PERMITS THE WRITE.  The write is AFTER the switch, so adding\n   `break` would still reach it.  **The proposed return matters.**\" ***  A `break`-shaped repair\n   would look right and do nothing -- exactly the sketch-versus-enclosing-control-flow rule this\n   project minted after four readers proposed a `return` into a function with a common tail.\n\n*** THE OBSERVER EXISTS, AND IT IS DISQUALIFIED BY THE PROBE'S OWN EXECUTION MODE. ***\n\nThe fork has debugger WRITE-WATCHPOINTS, and the common memory path checks them **before**\ndevice/cache/RAM dispatch -- *\"neither `PHYSICAL` nor `NO_EXCEPTIONS` excludes this helper's\nwrite\"* (`memory_rw.c:273`).  A matching attempt prints `WATCHPOINT: write`, which a pty driver can\ncount with no instrumented build.  **But two conditions kill it as written:**\n\n    memory_rw.c:273   the check requires  !single_step\n                      -- and sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py's run_ops() uses `step'.  Adding\n                      watchpoints to that scheme \"could report ZERO DESPITE WRITES.\"\n\n    same check        also requires  !about_to_enter_single_step, and SETS that flag after its\n                      first report -- so subsequent pixel writes inside the SAME drawing command\n                      go unreported.\n\n> **This is the carrier's single-stepping rule arriving on a new mechanism.**  It already records\n> that a stepped probe cannot observe interrupt DELIVERY (`cpu_dyntrans.c`: *\"Do not cause\n> interrupts while single-stepping\"*).  **Watchpoints are a second capability gated the same way**,\n> and the same discriminator applies: ask what the observation depends on before designing around\n> it.\n\n**WHAT THE MECHANISM CAN AND CANNOT SUPPORT, in codex's words:** it supports an\n**any-attempt-to-this-range** witness *\"when observation is active throughout the relevant\nexecution\"* -- i.e. FREE-RUNNING, not stepped -- and even then only the first report survives the\nflag.  It *\"cannot automatically support 'exactly N calls', or 'no writes anywhere from this\nhelper'.  A literal, attributed call count needs a suitable host-side observer or `harness:`\nwitness.\"*\n\n*** AND Q3 DEMOLISHES THE CHEAP ORACLE: \"unchanged destination bytes prove only unchanged OBSERVED\nCONTENTS.\" ***  Equality at the observation points is also consistent with a same-value write, a\nwrite followed by restoration, a write to somewhere else, no drawing operation at all, or an earlier\nhelper return.  **Two of those are live here rather than theoretical**: the helper returns on an\ninvalid tile pointer (`dev_sgi_re.c:274`), and *\"the memory path can discard an out-of-RAM write\nwhile returning success; corresponding reads can return zeros\"* (`memory_rw.c:662`).\n\n> **So a destination-equality row needs a control establishing that the pre-paint REALLY EXISTS and\n> that a SUPPORTED operation through the same path actually lands** -- otherwise \"unchanged\" is\n> indistinguishable from \"nothing ever happened\", which is the vacuity shape in its purest form.\n\n**THE TWO VIABLE PATHS, neither cheap:**\n* **(a) a FREE-RUNNING probe with a write-watchpoint** -- any-attempt semantics, first report only,\n  and it must abandon the stepped `run_ops()` idiom the sibling probes use;\n* **(b) a `harness:` witness with a host-side observer** -- an attributed call count, which the\n  ladder admits only when the round's subject IS the apparatus, so it would need care not to become\n  the rung-1 shortcut the ladder exists to refuse.\n\n**STATUS: this row is NOT held for fable5 and is the only HIGH row that is not** -- but its blocker\nis now a characterised capability gap rather than an open question, and neither path is a session's\nwork.  *Recorded in full because most of this analysis had been sitting unread in the artifact since\nit was produced, which is this session's most-repeated failure.*\n\n*** DESIGN PASS 1, 2026-09-08.  FIVE SEATS, UNANIMOUS ON BOTH QUESTIONS -- and the strongest answer\nREFUSED TO CERTIFY ITSELF, which is the part worth keeping. ***\n\n**THE DISPOSITION: member 3, DECLINE + LATCHED ANNOUNCE, implemented as candidate (b) -- skip the\nPUT's `memory_rw` for a declined mode.  Never an early `return`.**\n\n*** grok's REFRAMING IS BETTER THAN MY OPTION LIST AND IT DISSOLVES THE QUESTION THE ROW WAS FILED\nON. ***  The row asked *\"what should a declined BUFFER FILL leave behind?\"*, treating `buf` as the\nanalogue of `#504`'s `*color`.  It is not:\n\n> *\"Destination of a PUT is guest memory at `tileptr + ofs`, not `buf`.  `#504` declined the\n> helper's OUTPUT.  The PUT output is the store.  Skipping the store leaves whatever the guest\n> already had -- deterministic, no invented colour, no host bytes.  `buf` can stay uninitialised\n> because it is not sent.\"*\n\nSo the fix is not a fill policy at all.  **The question \"what should the buffer contain\" was the\nwrong question**, and my option (a) (zero-fill) was demoted to a fallback for exactly one case:\nif some tail consumes `buf` or assumes the store happened.\n\n**AND IT CORRECTED MY (a) IN PASSING:** zero-filling only `buf[0..3]` to mirror the `random()` shape\nis NOT (a) -- `bufdepth` can be 8, so that form *\"is this arm already instantiating\n`sgirebufuninit`\"*.  If (a) is ever taken it must define the whole payload.\n\n*** IT DECLINED TO CERTIFY (b), NAMING EXACTLY THE GAP: *** *\"That tail is not in the packet.  I\nwill not guess `x++`, a dirty bit, a return value, or a use of `buf`.  I cannot certify (b) against\n'nine lines down.'\"*  That is this project's own sketch rule, applied by a seat to its own\nrecommendation, unprompted beyond the caution in the brief.\n\n## *** BOTH OF ITS UNCHECKABLE PREMISES ARE NOW SETTLED BY ME, BY OPENING THE FILE ***\n\n**1. THERE IS NO COMMON TAIL.**  `horrible_getputpixel()` is `void`, and after the PUT store the\n`else` runs the GET path and the function ENDS -- `}` closing the else, `}` closing the function,\nnothing between them.  No dirty bit, no counter, no return value, no later use of `buf`.\n**So (b) is safe**, and the `put_ok` flag form grok gave is belt-and-braces rather than necessary --\nwhich is still the right way to write it, because it cannot become the early-`return` trap under a\nlater edit.\n\n**2. `fatal()` DOES NOT EXIT.**  `src/core/debugmsg.c`: `va_start; va_debug; va_end;` and return --\nno `exit()`, no early-out.  grok flagged that if `fatal()` aborted, *\"the random fill is dead and\nthe guest-visible row collapses.\"*  It does not abort, so **the random bytes really do reach\n`memory_rw(..., MEM_WRITE)` and the defect stands.**\n\n*That premise should have been IN the packet.  The carrier records `fatal()`'s non-exit as a\nstanding fact and I did not inline it -- the settled-premises rule exists precisely so a packet-fed\nseat need not park on something already known.*\n\n## ROUND SCOPE: TWO ROUNDS, 5 of 5, each with its own reason\n\nNot one round with `sgirebufuninit`, despite the two sitting in the same `switch`.  **minimax gave\nthe sharpest form:** *\"a regression in the CI/depth-8 path and a regression in the YCRCB path\nproduce the same test failure\"* -- i.e. one oracle cannot tell them apart, which is exactly the\nround-scope rule's test rather than a preference.  grok: *\"They sit in the same `switch`.  That is\nnot the test.\"*  **And it named the trap explicitly: zero-initialising `buf` at its declaration\nwould silently convert this round's (b) into (a) and smuggle `sgirebufuninit`'s fix in with it.**\n\n*This was asked as an open question arguing both ways, so the unanimity is worth something -- unlike\nunanimity on a brief-supplied FACT, which this project has measured to be worth nothing.*\n\n## RESIDUALS RAISED, NOT YET DISPOSED\n\n* grok: the arm *\"is already confused -- it announces unimplemented (`TODO` + `fatal`) and then\n  IMPLEMENTS with noise.  The announce is the right instinct (unlatched, per-pixel, therefore wrong\n  shape).  The fill is the defect.\"*  So the existing `fatal()` becomes the latched announce; it is\n  not added, it is fixed.\n* **12 of 16 mode values reach `default:` and only `0xf0` is documented.**  grok accepted the\n  arithmetic (`16 - 4`, needing no header) but declined the reachability claim as *\"claims about\n  other code, not in the packet\"* -- correctly.  **A round taking this owes the reachability\n  check**, and `IMPLEMENT` (member 7) for YCRCB is a separate design: *\"no YCbCr packing,\n  subsample, or byte order in this packet; 11 of the 12 values are not even named.\"*\n* Q4/Q5 (detector and witness) are in the seat answers and NOT yet consolidated here -- that is an\n  UNREAD-REVIEW debt on this row until it is.\n\n*** THE Q4/Q5 UNREAD-REVIEW DEBT IS DISCHARGED, AND IT WAS WORTH PAYING: THE PANEL'S OWN MAJORITY\nDETECTOR WOULD HAVE SHIPPED AN ESCAPABLE ROW. ***\n\nI recorded the detector and witness answers as unread.  Reading all five:\n\n## THE DETECTOR -- convergent, and it is a DESTINATION CANARY, not \"looks non-random\"\n\nPre-fill the target bytes with a distinctive non-zero canary, execute ONE PUT with a declined mode,\nread back, and **pass iff the canary is bit-identical** -- i.e. the oracle is *the store did not\nhappen*, not *the value is not random*.  A liveness control in the SAME script repeats with a\ndefined mode (RGBA or CI) and the canary MUST change; without it a dead PUT path greens the round.\n\n*** THE HOLE, and three of the five seats were in it: DO NOT PIN THE TEST TO `0xf0` ALONE. ***\ndeepseek and kimi both proposed YCRCB `0xf0` as *the* test mode, kimi arguing it is the value a real\nguest would plausibly select.  grok:\n\n> *\"A mutant `case 0xf0: put_ok = 0; break;` with `default:` still random would escape.  Use at\n> least one UNDOCUMENTED value (`0x40` is the first hole) as the primary; `0xf0` can be a second\n> vector, not the only one.\"*\n\n**That is a real escape against the majority design.**  The documented mode is the most tempting\nchoice and the weakest one, because a mutant can special-case exactly it.\n\n**FOUR MORE STRENGTHENINGS, one per seat, none of them duplicated:**\n* **grok** -- vary `bufdepth` over at least {1, 4, 8}, so a mutant that skips the store only when\n  `bufdepth == 4` dies.\n* **minimax** -- make the oracle **CONJUNCTIVE**: the latch transitioned 0 -> 1 *during that pixel*\n  (record the PRE-state) AND `memory_rw` was not called for it.  Either conjunct alone is escapable,\n  and recording the pre-state defeats a mutant that pre-sets the latch at init.\n* **kimi** -- assert the announce count is **exactly 1**, which is what actually pins the latch.\n* **glm** -- assert the ADJACENT bytes are unchanged too, since the write is only `bufdepth` bytes\n  at `ofs`; and run twice asserting identical transcripts.\n\n**AND grok'S HONEST LIMIT ON ITS OWN ORACLE:** keeping `buf[i] = random()` while setting\n`put_ok = 0` leaves the canary intact, so a destination-only oracle cannot see the leftover\n`random()` calls.  It calls that *\"a successful (b), not an escape, PROVIDED the patch also deletes\nthe four `random()` calls -- same edit, not a second oracle\"*, and explicitly declines to make\n\"`random()` is not called\" the oracle because an LCG or `rand()` defeats it.\n\n## *** THE BLOCKING PREMISE THREE SEATS RAISED INDEPENDENTLY IS NOW SETTLED -- BY ME, IN TWO READS,\n## AND IT SETTLES IN FAVOUR OF THE ROUND. ***\n\nglm, kimi and deepseek each named the SAME single fact as the one that decides whether a witness\nexists at all: **does the mode-register write path mask or reject `0xf0` (and the eleven\nundocumented values) BEFORE they reach this helper?**  glm put the consequence sharply: if it does,\n*\"no witness exists, the round is documented-only, and '12 of 16' then describes host-reachable\nstates no real guest can reach.\"*\n\n**THERE IS NO VALIDATION.**  `DEVICE_ACCESS(sgi_de)`:\n\n    :1098  /*  Treat all registers as read/write, by default. ... */\n    :1112  d->de_reg[regnr] = idata;              <- unconditional, unmasked\n\nand the `switch (relative_addr)` below it reaches `case CRIME_DE_MODE_DST:` only to `debug()` the\nvalue and `break` -- no mask, no rejection, no range test.  `draw_primitive()` then reads\n`d->de_reg[(CRIME_DE_MODE_DST - 0x2000)/4]` straight back, and `horrible_getputpixel()` computes\n`color_mode = mode & DE_MODE_TYPE_MASK`.\n\n> **So a guest store of ANY 32-bit value to CRIME_DE_MODE_DST is kept verbatim, and bits 4-7 select\n> among all 16 colour modes -- twelve of which reach the `random()` arm.  The witness is\n> constructible and the round is NOT documented-only.**\n\n*The file's own comment is the authority here, which is the strongest form: it is not that I failed\nto find a validator, it is that the code states it treats these registers as raw read/write.*\n\n**THE REMAINING UNSETTLED ITEM is smaller than it looked:** kimi asked whether `#504`'s witness\nitself selected a declined mode.  That is a question about a shipped probe, answerable by reading\n`regress/sgi_re_randompixel_probe.py`, and it is no longer load-bearing now that the register path\nis settled.",

